"""
GLE / VARX CURRENCY FORECASTING PIPELINE

======================================================================
1. WHAT THE PROGRAM DOES (ONE PARAGRAPH)
======================================================================
It treats the daily USD/INR log-return as a noisy, driven signal with finite
memory. A sparse linear map predicts tomorrow's return from (i) the last p
returns, (ii) recent realized volatility and (iii) today's returns of seven
global market series. The part of the return this map cannot explain is
modelled as a random force whose strength changes with time and whose tails
are heavy (GARCH(1,1) with Student-t innovations). The fitted model is then
(a) run forward thousands of times to produce a predictive distribution of
returns and prices, and (b) scored out of sample, against a random walk, with
statistics that are valid for nested models.

======================================================================
2. PHYSICAL PICTURE
======================================================================
The exchange rate is ONE OBSERVED DEGREE OF FREEDOM of a very large, open,
driven, dissipative system (the global market). Two quantities are resolved:

    r(t)     = ln P(t) - ln P(t-1)                         (log-return)
    sigma(t) = std( r(t-w+1), ..., r(t) ),  w = VOL_WINDOW   (realized volatility)

Every other degree of freedom (order flow, information, central-bank action,
other instruments) is either (i) observed and supplied as an external drive
x_j(t), or (ii) unobserved and eliminated.

Eliminating degrees of freedom by Mori-Zwanzig projection yields, for the
retained variable, a Generalized Langevin Equation (GLE):

    dr/dt = - Int_0^t K(t-s) r(s) ds  +  SUM_j Omega_j x_j(t)  +  eta(t)

    K(t-s)  memory kernel. The eliminated variables relax on finite time
            scales, so the past of r acts back on its present: r alone is
            non-Markovian. (A memoryless Langevin equation is the special case
            K(t-s) ~ delta(t-s).)
    Omega_j linear response of r to the external forcing x_j.
    eta(t)  projected unresolved force: the effect of all eliminated variables
            that is not a linear function of the retained history. It plays the
            role of the "random force" of Brownian motion.

Nothing here assumes equilibrium: no fluctuation-dissipation relation ties K
to the autocovariance of eta, no detailed balance is imposed, and no Markov
property is assumed. The two structural requirements are (a) weak stationarity
of r(t) over the sample (checked by an ADF test) and (b) a conditional mean
that is linear in its regressors with time-invariant coefficients over each
estimation window.

======================================================================
3. DISCRETE MODEL (one INR trading day = one time step)
======================================================================
Sampling the GLE on the daily grid turns the memory integral into a finite sum
and gives a linear autoregression with exogenous inputs (VARX):

    r(t+1) = SUM_{k=1..p} K_k r(t-k+1)  +  c sigma(t)  +  SUM_j Omega_j x_j(t)  +  eta(t)

(Signs and the discretization constants are absorbed into K_k.) Every
regressor is known at the close of day t, so this is a strictly causal
one-step predictor. {K_k} is the discretized memory kernel and p = MAX_LAG its
maximal depth; sigma(t) is an observed state variable, a nonlinear functional
of recent r that is allowed to shift the conditional mean.

Identifiability. From one trajectory, linear memory K and linear
autocorrelation of eta are indistinguishable: both appear as linear temporal
dependence. {K_k} is therefore the TOTAL linear temporal dependence of the
projected dynamics, and eta(t) is the innovation left after all linear
dependence on the past and on the drives has been removed.

======================================================================
4. ESTIMATION (sparse, noise-consistent linear regression)
======================================================================
All coefficients beta = (K_1..K_p, c, Omega_1..Omega_J) are estimated jointly
by weighted elastic net on z-scored regressors:

    min_beta  (1/2n) sum_t w_t (y_t - b0 - Z_t beta)^2
              + alpha [ rho ||beta||_1 + (1-rho)/2 ||beta||_2^2 ]

    y = r(t+1);  Z = column-standardized regressors;  rho = l1_ratio;
    w_t = observation weights (see below).

* Standardization makes the penalty independent of measurement units. The
  reported coefficients are STANDARDIZED, beta_std = beta_raw * sd(regressor):
  the change in r(t+1) per one-sd change of the regressor.
* The L1 term sets weak coefficients exactly to zero. The surviving set
  {j : Omega_j != 0} is the estimated set of drives that LEAD r by one step (a
  statistical lead-lag relation, not a structural coupling); excluded
  regressors are absorbed into eta. Unused lags are likewise zeroed, so the
  EFFECTIVE memory depth is the largest lag index with nonzero K_k. The L2 term
  stabilizes the fit when regressors are correlated.
* (alpha, rho) are chosen by cross-validation on TimeSeriesSplit: every
  validation block lies strictly after its training block, so the selection
  never uses the future. The alpha grid is generated from the data, from the
  smallest alpha that yields the all-zero model downward in logarithmic steps,
  so it adapts to the scale of y.
* Weights. The noise is heteroskedastic and heavy-tailed (section 4), so the
  mean is estimated in two passes that make it consistent with the noise
  model. Pass 1 is unweighted; its residuals are fitted by GARCH(1,1)-t. Pass 2
  repeats the elastic net with the Student-t maximum-likelihood (IRLS) weights
  w_t = (nu+1) / ((nu-2+z_t^2) h_t),  z_t = eta_t/sqrt(h_t),  normalized to
  mean 1. Observations taken in high-noise states, and extreme residuals, are
  thereby down-weighted. The same estimator is used for the production fit and
  for every walk-forward refit.

======================================================================
5. NOISE MODEL
======================================================================
The residual eta(t) is modelled as GARCH(1,1) with unit-variance Student-t
innovations:

    eta(t) = sqrt(h(t)) z(t),   z(t) ~ standardized Student-t(nu)
    h(t)   = omega + alpha eta(t-1)^2 + beta h(t-1)

h(t) is the conditional variance, the instantaneous intensity of the random
force. Volatility clustering means that after a shock the noise amplitude
relaxes slowly: alpha+beta < 1 is the relaxation factor per day, the
unconditional variance is omega/(1-alpha-beta), and alpha+beta -> 1 is
near-integrated variance (shocks barely decay). nu sets the tail weight
(nu -> infinity is Gaussian). Parameters are obtained by maximum likelihood.

======================================================================
6. FORWARD SIMULATION (Monte Carlo)
======================================================================
Each simulated return depends on its own simulated past through three
channels: the lag buffer (K), the realized volatility sigma (a sample standard
deviation of the path's own returns) and the noise variance h (which depends
on the previous simulated noise). The law of r(t+H) therefore has no closed
form for H > 1 and is obtained by propagating an ensemble of sample paths
through the full recursion. Empirical quantiles over the ensemble approximate
the predictive distribution conditional on information at time T under the
fitted dynamics. Cumulative log-returns are path sums; prices are
P(T) exp(cumulative return).

Scope: the ensemble represents uncertainty from the noise process and from
future drive values. Fitted coefficients are treated as fixed, and future
drives are sampled independently of the simulated path.

======================================================================
7. EVALUATION
======================================================================
Walk-forward (expanding window). At each refit, using only data before the
prediction date, the weighted elastic net (including its hyperparameters) is
re-fitted and used for the next REFIT_EVERY one-step predictions.

Mean accuracy is measured against two benchmarks: the zero forecast E[r]=0
(random walk in price without drift) and the expanding-mean forecast (random
walk with drift, built only from past data). Every competitor nests its
benchmark (setting its extra coefficients to zero recovers the benchmark), so
equal accuracy is tested with the one-sided Clark-West (2007) statistic,

    f_t = (y-b)^2 - [(y-a)^2 - (b-a)^2] = 2 (y-b)(a-b)

(y outcome, b benchmark forecast, a larger-model forecast) with HAC variance.
The correction term (b-a)^2 removes the upward bias that estimating
coefficients whose true value is zero adds to a model's squared error, which a
plain squared-error comparison would misread as lack of skill. Reported:
RMSE, MAE, out-of-sample R2 = 1 - SSE_model/SSE_zero, and the Clark-West
p-values (model vs zero; candidate vs drift; candidate vs the no-memory model).

The predictive DENSITY (the object that is simulated) is evaluated one step
ahead out of sample: at every refit the GARCH-t noise model is re-fitted on
the data before the block and then filtered through the block using only past
errors and frozen parameters. The probability integral transforms (PIT) are
tested for uniformity (Kolmogorov-Smirnov), 5%/95% tail coverage is reported,
and the mean log score is compared with a constant-variance Gaussian.

Adequacy of the model's truncations is tested on the filtered standardized
errors z by Ljung-Box (remaining linear dependence), ARCH-LM (remaining
conditional heteroskedasticity) and CUSUM (stability of the mean of z).

======================================================================
INPUTS
======================================================================
  usd_inr.csv        date, price
  market_drives.csv  date, and <name>_r (daily log return) for each of
                     broad_usd, brent_oil, gold, vix, sp500, nikkei, us10y

USAGE
  python gle_pipeline.py
  python gle_pipeline.py --csv usd_inr.csv --market market_drives.csv --outdir results
  python gle_pipeline.py --demo            # synthetic data with known ground truth
  python gle_pipeline.py --horizon 60 --n-sims 5000 --start 2019-01-01 --end 2025-06-30

REQUIREMENTS  scikit-learn >= 1.7 (ElasticNetCV(alphas=<int>)), arch, statsmodels, scipy

OUTPUTS (--outdir)
  features.csv  residuals.csv  simulation_forecast.csv  evaluation_report.txt
  model.pkl     pipeline_summary.json
"""

